.TH YES 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
yes \- write a line repeatedly until stopped
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yes
.RI [ string ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yes
writes the same line to standard output over and over.
The line is its arguments, separated by single spaces, followed by a
newline.
With no arguments, the line is
.BR y .
It reads no input.
This
.B yes
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
The arguments are written byte for byte, except to a console; see
.BR NOTES .
A backslash is an ordinary character, and
.B yes
interprets no escape sequences itself.
Every argument counts, even an empty one: two empty arguments give a line
with one space, and a single empty argument, as in
.BR "yes \(aq\(aq" ,
gives empty lines.
.PP
.B yes
does not end on its own.
It runs until it is interrupted or killed, such as by Ctrl+C, until its
output is a pipe that is closed, or until it notices that a write
failed; see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.SH OPTIONS
.B yes
has no options other than
.BR \-\-help .
Every other argument is part of the line, including
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-\- ,
and other words that begin with a dash.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the busybox version and a usage message on standard output, and
exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
In any other position it is part of the line.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B yes
reads no environment variables itself.
The startup code and the console output code shared by all applets read
these, which can change what
.B yes
writes:
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ", " BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION
When standard output is a console, decide whether the console or busybox
interprets the escape sequences in the arguments, such as for color.
See
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B CONEMUPID
When it is set and neither of the above is, busybox uses terminal mode
0: it turns virtual terminal output off and emulates escape sequences
itself.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
The startup code also reads
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
which do not change what
.B yes
does.
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The first argument was
.BR \-\-help .
No other way of ending
.B yes
gives status 0.
.TP
.B 1
A write to standard output failed, for a reason other than a closed
pipe.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has closed it.
Nothing is written to standard error.
.PP
A failed write is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
yes: : \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
such as
.BR "yes: : Bad file descriptor" ;
the doubled colon is part of the message.
A failed write is not always noticed; see
.BR NOTES .
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a line three times, then stop by ending the pipe:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ yes \(aqhello world\(aq | head \-n 3
hello world
hello world
hello world
.EE
.RE
.PP
Answer yes to every question that a command asks on standard input:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ yes | cp \-i new.txt old.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create a file of one million octets, made of lines holding
.BR y :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ yes | head \-c 1000000 >y.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create a file of 100 empty lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ yes \(aq\(aq | head \-n 100 >blank.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Words that look like options are written like any other:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ yes \-\- \-n | head \-n 1
\-\- \-n
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR yes :
.IP \(bu 2
No word is an option, except
.B \-\-help
as the first argument.
.B \-\-
does not end options: it is written like any other argument.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-\-version .
.PP
Standard output is binary, so every line ends with a bare LF, not
CR LF.
.PP
A write error is noticed only when it happens while the newline is
written.
The C library writes out its 4096-byte output buffer when the buffer
overflows, and reports a failure only to the call that overflowed it,
which may be the call that writes the text of the line or a space.
The error is then lost and
.B yes
runs on with its output discarded.
For a line shorter than 4096 bytes, counting the newline, the newline
sooner or later overflows the buffer if the length is odd, and never if
it is even.
For example, with standard output closed, as with
.BR >&\- ,
or a file opened only for reading,
.B "yes ab"
and
.B "yes abcd"
fail with status 1, but
.BR yes ,
.BR "yes x" ,
and
.B "yes abc"
never stop.
A closed pipe is noticed whichever call hits it, because the Windows
output code of busybox then exits the process with status 141 at once.
.PP
Arguments may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903
and later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
When standard output is a console, the text of the arguments is converted
for display from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version
1903 and later.
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates the common ones on consoles without virtual terminal support.
.PP
When
.B yes
is run from
.BR cmd.exe ,
the C runtime expands wildcards in the arguments, even quoted ones, so
.B yes *.c
writes the names of the matching files; set
.B BB_GLOBBING
to
.B 0
to prevent that.
.PP
In the kit's
.BR sh (1),
.B yes
is an applet that busybox does not mark
.BR NOFORK ,
so the shell runs it in another busybox process, not inside itself.
That way Ctrl+C can end it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR echo (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR sh (1)
